  "id": "COMPOUND:16166 - PROTEIN:5787",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Traxillagenin may interact with RXF 631 through multiple distinct pathways. In Path 1, Traxillagenin is linked to CD3, a T-cell surface glycoprotein, which is targeted by Phyllofolactones F, and Phyllofolactones F in turn targets RXF 631. In Path 2, Traxillagenin targets M059J, which is a target of Lup-20(29)-en-3-ol, (3beta)-, and this compound also targets RXF 631. Path 3 shows that Traxillagenin targets CD3, which is targeted by Oppsit-4(15)-Ene-1Beta,11-Diol, which also targets RXF 631. These findings collectively indicate that Traxillagenin may act as a modulator of RXF 631 through different molecular mechanisms involving T-cell surface proteins. The evidence is consistent across multiple pathways, suggesting a potential role for Traxillagenin in influencing RXF 631 through diverse biological processes. The strength of the evidence is moderate, as the scores are relatively similar across the paths, but the exact mechanism of interaction remains uncertain, requiring further experimental validation.",
